# Prospective relational planning control properties

Author: actual `/root/research_architect`. This NEW public design preserves `schemas/SEMANTICS.md` SHA256 `718cd0da46f9fb2beaf179526a8c089a69c892cba099632aa6b3af3002371bdd`. It defines prospective evidence obligations, not new action dynamics, targets, generated examples or approval. Graph fuel is consumed and never replenished; block moves have only the stated clear/source/destination preconditions. Resource transfer, inventory, unlocking, scheduling and edit-distance repair are outside this wave.

## Units and factual row properties

A source family contains one fresh base relational world plus its declared controlled interventions, plans and renderers. Changing a budget, deleting an edge, extending a partial goal, changing a plan or reordering JSON inside that family adds no independent family. A different key alone proves no novelty. Base-world isomorphism and custody checks below are required.

Optional row tags may be added to the existing `skill_tags` list only when the corresponding witness is recomputed from that row. A family contrast does not authorize tagging every member with the property it demonstrates.

| Property | Exact factual witness |
|---|---|
| `legal_prefix_then_first_failure` | Submitted plan has a first illegal action at index `i >= 1`; every earlier action is legal. Trace stops at `i`, retains the previous state and evaluates no suffix. |
| `fuel_lengthens_fresh_minimum_moves` | Initial goal has a finite minimum feasible move length strictly greater than the directed topology's minimum move length when fuel is ignored. Both minima are independently searched. |
| `fuel_blocks_topologically_reachable_goal` | Ignoring fuel there is a path to goal, but no path within initial fuel. Exhaust finite `(node, remaining_fuel)` states; a timeout is not a witness. |
| `valid_goal_detour` | Entire submitted plan is legal and goal reaching; its length is strictly greater than the independently verified minimum move length. This includes block tasks. |
| `later_action_requires_changed_block_clearance` | At submitted index `i >= 1`, the action is legal after its actual prefix and illegal in the initial state because its source or destination is not clear. Record which precondition and the actual preceding move that changed the relevant clearance. An initially illegal action alone is insufficient. |
| `partial_block_goal` | The valid nonempty `goal_on` map specifies fewer blocks than the initial `on` map. No assumption is made about unspecified supports. |
| `required_temporary_goal_displacement` | At least one explicit goal relation is initially true, a goal plan exists, and the finite state search forbidding violation of **all** initially true goal relations cannot reach the goal. This proves some initially satisfied goal fact must be temporarily broken; it does not identify one particular fact as individually necessary. |

Witnesses may use public input facts, action indices and independently derived measurements in a separate audit. Gold values are not metadata hints in student prompts. No new canonical top-level fields are required by this proposal.

## Four prospective family strata and four contexts

All contexts inherit the same lineage key and split. Context names are internal and excluded from rendered input. The producer must construct tasks meeting these constraints, then derive labels using the unchanged contract; these definitions supply no literal worlds or expected answers.

**A — decisive fuel controls (12 new graph families).** Context A0 is a graph with a legal goal-reaching submitted plan; A1 changes **only initial fuel**, keeping all names, edges, start/goal and the entire submitted plan identical. Six families witness topologically reachable but fuel-infeasible initial goals at A1; use the exact minimum required fuel boundary and its one-unit lower neighbor. Six witness a finite A1 minimum move length strictly greater than A0's minimum, with A0's submitted route blocked at A1 and a longer feasible fresh route available. A2 is a nonempty legal non-goal submitted prefix in the A0 world. A3 has an illegal missing directed edge after a legal prefix in the A0 world. A2/A3 must include matched submitted lengths across the actual stratum where feasible, rather than making length the sole outcome cue.

**B — decisive directed-edge controls (12 new graph families).** B0 submits a legal goal-reaching plan of at least three moves. B1 deletes exactly one existing directed edge used after a legal prefix, with every other task field and the entire plan identical. Both worlds must retain some feasible fresh goal plan, so removing the edge changes submitted legality without collapsing into an initial-unreachability label. B2 demonstrates an attempted reverse traversal of an existing edge whose reverse is absent, after a legal prefix. B3 is a legal goal-reaching detour in the B0 world. No direction symmetry is inferred. At least half the B0 worlds have two minimum-move goal plans so complete-sequence lexical tie handling is exercised; this is measured, not inferred from branching.

**C — block clearance and ordering (12 new block families).** C0 is a legal goal-reaching minimum-move plan of four to eight moves, including `later_action_requires_changed_block_clearance`. C1 exchanges two adjacent C0 actions while preserving the full plan length and all world/goal facts; independently replayed C1 must be illegal because of a clear-source/destination dependency. First-error index must vary across families; a fixed index is not a coverage claim. C2 is a legal goal-reaching detour in exactly the C0 world. C3 is a nonempty legal prefix ending short of the goal. At least four C0 worlds witness required temporary goal displacement; if that witness fails, change the proposed world before freezing, not the tag definition.

**D — partial-goal obligation controls (12 new block families).** D0 supplies a valid nonempty proper partial goal and a legal goal-reaching minimum plan of at least two moves. D1 changes **only `goal_on`** by adding valid explicit obligations; the same submitted plan stays legal but fails the strengthened goal, and the fresh minimum is strictly longer. D2 supplies a legal minimum plan for the D1 goal. D3 supplies a valid goal-reaching detour for the D1 goal. Goal extensions must remain well formed; contradictory goals are rejected input, not negative training labels.

## Public family manifest interface

If this pilot is implemented, its NEW control manifest has top-level `version`, `track_id`, `batch_name`, `semantics_sha256` and `families`. Each family entry has `source_family_key`, `source_index`, `split`, `primary_stratum` (`A|B|C|D`), `base_sample_id`, `contexts` and `controls`. Each context has `context_id`, `sample_ids`, `row_properties` and `row_witnesses`. `sample_ids` includes both renderer views; a row witness has `sample_id`, `property`, `scope="row"` and `evidence` containing explicit indices/input fact identifiers or independently verified scalar measurements. Properties are optional per row and cannot be inherited merely from the family.

Each control has `control_id`, `name`, `scope="corpus_variant"`, `sample_ids`, `changed_task_fields`, and `provenance="supplied_tasks"`. The control names are `fuel_only_counterfactual`, `directed_edge_deletion_counterfactual`, `adjacent_action_order_counterfactual`, `partial_goal_extension_counterfactual` and `same_world_valid_goal_detour`. The named pair must appear in the supplied corpus, not only in a verification probe. A fuel-only pair changes exactly `fuel`; an edge pair changes exactly `edges` by one deleted triple; order pairs change exactly `plan` by one adjacent swap; goal pairs change exactly `goal_on` by valid extension. Detours change exactly `plan` and preserve legal goal achievement while increasing length. `changed_task_fields` must be verified against parsed payloads.

Audit-only counterfactuals use a separate `scope="audit_probe"` evidence list with actual invocation/completion, input hashes and comparisons. They do not replace supplied controls or add families. Formal/expert/red reports bind exact frozen candidate and public specification hashes. No private expected-value field or unpublished evidence-ID uniqueness assumption is required; a supplied pair can support multiple truthful properties.

## Independent oracle and quality obligations before expansion

1. Independently implement graph replay, finite `(node,fuel)` search and complete-sequence lexical minimum; separately check cheapest fuel cost, topology-only distance and minimum-move route multiplicity. Cheapest fuel is a constraint diagnostic, never the target objective.
2. Independently implement block relation-set replay and exhaustive finite-state BFS. Clear objects and unchanged supports must be checked at each intermediate state. Validate goal well-formedness before search. Compare a separately written transition implementation on public hand-built fixtures before any production label is seen.
3. Independently test permutation equivariance of names, object/list/key order read-back, stop-at-first-illegal suffix absorption, exact one-unit fuel boundaries, partial-goal extension monotonicity of minimum length, and lexical tie changes under renaming. Renaming preserves legality and minimum length; a canonical tie plan may change because lexical order changes.
4. Measure actual source-family novelty, field differences, all four context obligations, row property witnesses, split custody and renderer equality. Inspect all 48 families in completed expert/red reviews; freeze input-derived predictions before targets. Later adjudication is separate and cannot replace missing first review evidence.
5. Report solver state counts, maximum frontier and completion. Six-block raw support assignments are bounded by `6^6`; valid configurations are a subset. Graph pilot nodes are at most seven and fuel at most 30, giving at most 217 `(node,fuel)` states. These bounds do not establish that every sampled task is difficult. Incomplete search is `UNKNOWN`, never infeasible.

The design author previously adjudicated approved RGM releases and may later adjudicate this pilot after independent first reviews. That design/adjudication overlap and the common underlying model must be disclosed; different agent executions do not prove independent error distributions.
